  },
  {
    question: 'Should I take notes in Markdown?',
    answer: 'Yes, <strong>Markdown</strong> is increasingly popular for note-taking because it\'s: portable (plain text works everywhere), future-proof (won\'t be locked to one app), fast to write (no formatting toolbar needed), and widely supported. <strong>Obsidian</strong>, <strong>Logseq</strong>, <strong>Bear</strong>, and <strong>Craft</strong> all use Markdown. <strong>Notion</strong> uses Markdown-like syntax but stores in proprietary format.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I sync my notes across devices?',
    answer: 'Yes, most note apps offer cloud sync: <strong>Notion</strong>, <strong>Evernote</strong>, <strong>Roam</strong>, <strong>Mem</strong>, <strong>Apple Notes</strong> (iCloud), <strong>OneNote</strong> (OneDrive) all sync automatically. <strong>Obsidian</strong> and <strong>Logseq</strong> require paid sync ($8-10/mo) or third-party services. <strong>Bear</strong> requires Pro subscription ($1.49/mo) for sync.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a Zettelkasten and do I need it?',
    answer: '<strong>Zettelkasten</strong> is a note-taking method focusing on atomic notes (one idea per note) connected via links to build a "second brain." Apps like <strong>Obsidian</strong>, <strong>Roam Research</strong>, and <strong>Logseq</strong> are designed for this approach. You don\'t need it - simple folders and search work fine for most people. But if you\'re a researcher, writer, or knowledge worker building complex ideas, Zettelkasten is powerful.'
